<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{ grav.language.getActive ?: theme.default_lang|default('en') }}">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>{% if page.title %}{{ page.title|e('html') }} | {% endif %}{{ site.title|e('html') }}</title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <meta name="description" content="{{ site.metadata.description }}">

    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Libre+Caslon+Display&family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">

    {% do assets.addCss('theme://css/style.css', 100) %}
    {{ assets.css()|raw }}
</head>
<body class="{{ page.header.body_classes }}">

    <header class="site-header">
        <nav class="main-navigation">
            <div class="logo">
                <a href="{{ base_url == '' ? '/' : base_url }}">{{ site.title }}</a>
            </div>
            <button class="nav-toggle" aria-label="Toggle navigation">
                <span></span>
                <span></span>
                <span></span>
            </button>
            {% include 'partials/navigation.html.twig' %}
        </nav>
    </header>

    {% if page.home %}
        <div id="teaser">
            <canvas id="teaser-canvas"></canvas>
        </div>
    {% else %}
        <div class="page-title-header">
            <h1 class="page-title">{{ page.title }}</h1>
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    <main id="main-content">
        {% block content %}
            {{ page.content|raw }}
        {% endblock %}
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
        <div class="main-footer">
            <span class="footer-copyright">&copy; {{ "now"|date("Y") }} Kaspar 2028</span>
        </div>
    </footer>

    <script>
        const toggle = document.querySelector('.nav-toggle');
        const navList = document.querySelector('.nav-list');
        if (toggle && navList) {
            toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
                toggle.classList.toggle('open');
                navList.classList.toggle('open');
            });
            navList.querySelectorAll('a').forEach(a => {
                a.addEventListener('click', () => {
                    toggle.classList.remove('open');
                    navList.classList.remove('open');
                });
            });
        }
    </script>

    {% if page.home %}
        <script>
            const TEASER_TEXT = `Peter Handke's play Kaspar remains one of the most radical and destabilizing works of modern drama because it treats language not as a gift but as a structural violence. It begins with a figure who is a literal blank slate, a personification of raw human existence devoid of social programming, who enters the stage with a single, repetitive sentence that he does not yet understand. This character is modeled loosely on the historical Kaspar Hauser, but Handke strips away the nineteenth-century mystery to focus entirely on the mechanics of linguistic indoctrination. Throughout the duration of the play, Kaspar is subjected to a relentless barrage of voices from unseen prompters who function as the architects of his consciousness. These voices do not merely teach him to communicate; they teach him to categorize, to judge, and to conform to a pre-existing social order. Handke utilizes a technique he calls speech torture to illustrate how the acquisition of grammar is simultaneously the acquisition of a mental cage. As Kaspar learns to name the objects around him, he loses his direct, unmediated relationship with reality. He learns that a table is a table and that it must be placed in a certain way, and in doing so, he internalizes the rules of a society that demands efficiency and predictability over individual essence. The tragedy of the play lies in its depiction of progress as a form of erasure. By the time Kaspar becomes a fluent speaker, he has been hollowed out, becoming a mere echo of the prompters who have colonized his mind. The play is physically and auditorily exhausting, designed to make the audience feel the weight of this linguistic imposition. Handke's rhythmic and repetitive prose creates a sense of claustrophobia, suggesting that our very thoughts are composed of borrowed parts and inherited structures. By the final act, when multiple versions of Kaspar appear on stage, the individuality of the protagonist has been completely liquidated into a mass-produced social identity. The play ends not in a triumph of human expression, but in a cacophony of noise and a breakdown of the very order that was so painstakingly constructed.`;
        </script>
        <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/matter-js/0.19.0/matter.min.js"></script>
        <script src="{{ url('theme://js/teaser.js') }}"></script>
    {% endif %}

</body>
</html>
